1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:03,520
Detaching from a session already
keeps your agents running,

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:06,160
because the daemon owns
them, not your terminal.

3
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:08,800
The harder case is stopping
the session outright.

4
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:11,840
So: two stacked panes,
and Claude Code started

5
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:13,920
in the first one with hash hash claude.

6
00:00:16,385 --> 00:00:19,185
The second pane gets Codex, the same way.

7
00:00:19,265 --> 00:00:21,985
Two different agents,
two panes, one session.

8
00:00:21,985 --> 00:00:24,865
Rysh pins each one to
the pane it started in,

9
00:00:24,865 --> 00:00:27,185
and remembers which
conversation belongs where.

10
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:33,160
Now something to prove it by. Each agent
is told a codeword and nothing else.

11
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:35,880
Only these two running
conversations know it.

12
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:38,920
Nothing on disk in this
project mentions either word.

13
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:45,460
And now the session is stopped.
Not detached — stopped.

14
00:01:45,620 --> 00:01:50,740
The daemon exits, every pane shell is
killed, and both agent processes go with it.

15
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:53,620
The session is still listed,
but nothing of it is running.

16
00:01:56,975 --> 00:02:01,055
Start it again, and the layout comes back
— which multiplexers have always done.

17
00:02:01,935 --> 00:02:03,215
Watch the panes though.

18
00:02:03,215 --> 00:02:08,480
Rysh replays each agent's own resume line,
so Claude and Codex restart by themselves,

19
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:11,360
in the right directories, unattended
and without being prompted.

20
00:02:43,165 --> 00:02:47,245
The real question is whether the
conversation came back or just the process.

21
00:02:47,805 --> 00:02:53,085
So ask. Each one answers with the
codeword it was told before the stop,

22
00:02:53,085 --> 00:02:55,165
which means these are the
same two conversations,

23
00:02:55,165 --> 00:02:59,430
continued, not two fresh agents that
happen to be sitting in the same panes.

